WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.901 --> 00:00:05.338
NARRATOR: The Bermuda Triangle
contains some of the deepest

00:00:05.405 --> 00:00:07.407
trenches in the world.

00:00:07.474 --> 00:00:11.077
The Puerto Rico Trench at
the Triangle's southern point

00:00:11.144 --> 00:00:15.815
reaches depths of
over 27,000 feet.

00:00:15.882 --> 00:00:20.053
But Dr. Gallagher suspects that
Mabel may frequent the Tongue

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:23.156
of the Ocean, a steep
underwater trench

00:00:23.223 --> 00:00:26.259
less than a mile away
from Rose Island.

00:00:26.326 --> 00:00:29.195
The deepest branch of
the Great Bahama Canyon,

00:00:29.262 --> 00:00:32.532
it's named for its shape
that's like a tongue.

00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:37.537
The Tongue of the Ocean drops
off steeply just half a mile

00:00:37.604 --> 00:00:38.905
or so offshore.

00:00:38.972 --> 00:00:44.411
Goes from about 30 feet to 300
to thousands really quickly.

00:00:44.477 --> 00:00:46.913
We really have no
idea what's going on

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:48.648
down in the deep trenches here.

00:00:48.715 --> 00:00:50.517
Anything could be
happening in the deep sea.

00:00:50.583 --> 00:00:53.520
It's one of the final frontiers
for human exploration,

00:00:53.586 --> 00:00:57.390
even more interesting, in my
opinion, than exploring space.

00:00:57.457 --> 00:00:59.959
JOE ROMEIRO: So
animals of all sorts

00:01:00.026 --> 00:01:01.127
can live within this trench.

00:01:01.194 --> 00:01:02.562
And it seems like
there's reports

00:01:02.629 --> 00:01:04.864
of all kinds of different
creatures living

00:01:04.931 --> 00:01:06.499
within the tongue of the ocean.

00:01:06.566 --> 00:01:09.402
It's sort of become
this legendary spot.

00:01:11.805 --> 00:01:14.874
NARRATOR: If Mabel is
in the Bermuda trenches,

00:01:14.941 --> 00:01:18.244
she may not be alone.

00:01:18.311 --> 00:01:21.347
Once thought to be a barren
zone with few lifeforms

00:01:21.414 --> 00:01:24.584
able to sustain themselves
without the sun's light,

00:01:24.651 --> 00:01:27.787
marine biologists have
discovered that the deep waters

00:01:27.854 --> 00:01:32.091
of the Bermuda Triangle
are home to other sharks,

00:01:32.158 --> 00:01:37.664
from the megamouth to
the sharpnose sevengill.

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:40.400
Among the diverse shark
population in the depths

00:01:40.467 --> 00:01:44.037
of the Bermuda Triangle,
one small shark stands

00:01:44.103 --> 00:01:46.539
out for its audacious attacks--

00:01:46.606 --> 00:01:50.376
the cookiecutter shark,
a terrifying shark

00:01:50.443 --> 00:01:53.146
with a deceptively sweet name.

00:01:53.213 --> 00:01:54.681
SPEAKER: Cookiecutters
have very distinctive.

00:01:54.747 --> 00:01:56.616
Their teeth are
actually fused together.

00:01:56.683 --> 00:01:59.052
Each individual tooth is
fused together, unlike what

00:01:59.118 --> 00:02:00.386
you see with other sharks.

00:02:00.453 --> 00:02:02.822
NARRATOR: This rarely
photographed shark makes

00:02:02.889 --> 00:02:04.791
dramatic vertical migrations.

00:02:04.858 --> 00:02:09.362
During the day, it dwells
in depths over 3,000 feet.

00:02:09.429 --> 00:02:13.900
But at night, it rises up
over 2,000 feet to hunt.

00:02:13.967 --> 00:02:16.169
SPEAKER: If you look on the
underside of a cookiecutter

00:02:16.236 --> 00:02:18.404
shark, it actually has
cells that produce light,

00:02:18.471 --> 00:02:19.939
what's called bioluminescence.

00:02:20.006 --> 00:02:22.809
When a predator sees the
bioluminescence in the water,

00:02:22.876 --> 00:02:25.478
it goes over to it to see if
anything has been disturbed.

00:02:25.545 --> 00:02:28.014
A cookiecutter shark
uses this opportunity

00:02:28.081 --> 00:02:30.083
to latch onto the side
of these predators

00:02:30.149 --> 00:02:32.185
and take a bite from it.

00:02:32.252 --> 00:02:36.022
NARRATOR: The cookie cutter
only grows to 20 inches,

00:02:36.089 --> 00:02:39.325
yet it has the audacity
to attack whales,

00:02:39.392 --> 00:02:43.263
tuna, even great white sharks.

00:02:43.329 --> 00:02:45.565
The proof is in
the telltale injury

00:02:45.632 --> 00:02:47.901
the cookiecutter
leaves on its victim--

00:02:47.967 --> 00:02:52.171
a hole in the skin about this
size and shape of a cookie.

00:02:52.238 --> 00:02:53.773
If you ever looked
at how they get

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:56.476
cookie dough out of
something, it's with a scoop.

00:02:56.543 --> 00:02:58.478
And that's basically
what these sharks do.

00:02:58.545 --> 00:03:00.313
They have this
jaw structure that

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:02.515
can latch onto the
side of an animal

00:03:02.582 --> 00:03:07.086
and like scoop out
a piece of flesh.

00:03:07.153 --> 00:03:09.255
NARRATOR: The
cookiecutter shark spends

00:03:09.322 --> 00:03:11.424
its days deep in the ocean.

00:03:11.491 --> 00:03:14.327
But even at shallower
depths, these sharks can be

00:03:14.394 --> 00:03:17.497
extremely difficult to locate.

